        "knowledge_point": "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
        "problem": "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int e^{1-3x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$",
        "worked_solution": "If we let $u=1-3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{1-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
